## Findings

**1 + 2 (HIGH/MEDIUM, ringbuffer + forward loop) — resolved.**
Ring-buffer gap recovery is now `(:first s)` — the contract's "earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained" — instead of `(min first visible-tail)`. When eviction has passed a frozen attachment's tail, recovery now points past that tail and the following read answers `end`, so recovery always makes progress. Regression test: `attachment-lifecycle-regressions` → "gap recovery from a frozen attachment whose history is fully evicted" (capacity 1, first=2 > frozen tail=1; asserts position 2 and `end`, not a second gap).

**2 (forward totality) — resolved, independently of the transport fix.** `forward-step` now carries a separate resume allowance equal to `:batch-budget`: a gap resume advances the cursor without forwarding, at most `budget` times, then returns `:continue` at the last recovery cursor. A recovery cursor equal to the cursor just read is treated as a transport fixed point and terminates as `:source-gap`. Tests: `gap-resume-is-bounded-and-total` (fixed-point source → one read, `:source-gap`; endlessly-retreating source, budget 3 → 4 reads, `:continue` at `{:pos 4}`). Both fixtures hang forever against the old code. The existing `gap-policy-is-explicit` semantics are unchanged (resume still reaches `:source-ended` with `forwarded 1`).

**3 (MEDIUM, pending handoff slots) — resolved.** `endpoint-step` opens with a reclamation sweep: any `:pending` slot whose handle state has reached `:closed` is released. That covers pre-accept peer close, wire protocol failure, and a composition closing the pending handle (which is also how an endpoint-wide stop is expressed with the current surface — close each pending handle, then step; I did not invent a `stop!` operation, since none exists in the staged surface). Slot release is now one `release-slot!` helper used by all five paths (offer-failed, accept, accept-failed, invalid-ack, expiry); it also drops the retained `:handle`/`:handle-state`, so a released slot's later acknowledgement is stale by construction. Bounded admission is preserved: tests assert `:ws/full` while an offer is outstanding, `:free` + reusable only after the sweep, and that a stale ack for a released slot sends no `:ws/accept` frame and deposits nothing.

**4 (LOW, `:ws/value nil`) — resolved.** `emit!` is now two arities (event / event+value) with a sibling `emit-reason!`; payload events always carry the `:ws/value` key, so `contains?` distinguishes "value nil" from "no value". `:ws/reason` stays optional. Existing envelope shapes are unchanged.

**5 (LOW, reader-conditional order) — resolved within authorized scope.** `:cljd` moved to the head in `ringbuffer.cljc` (atom instance check), the five `catch` conditionals in `ws.cljc`, the one in `rpc.cljc`, and the `:require`, `read-forms`, and `catch` in `v2_adapter.cljc`. Host behavior unchanged. **Deferred:** `apply.cljc:182` and `serving.cljc:37,157,195` carry the same LOW issue but are outside the authorized file list.

**6 (MEDIUM, concurrency oracle) — resolved.** `bounded-concurrent-linearizability-histories` (JVM-only, `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`) runs five bounded families on real handles through `conformance/record-op!` with threads released from a common gate: append/append, append/next, append/close, cursor/close on an attachment, and two independent attachment readers after one completed append. Each asserts `:linearizable? true`. `linearizability-oracle-rejects-an-invalid-history` seeds a genuine ordering violation (append completes, then a `blocked` read) and asserts `:linearizable? false` with `:reason :no-legal-linearization`; that one is host-neutral. I verified empirically that the recorded intervals do overlap (majority of runs) rather than serializing, and ran the ringbuffer suite 13× with no flakes.

**7 (LOW/INFO, rpc diagnostics + adapter) — resolved.** `rpc/take-diagnostics` mirrors `take-completed`, giving `:diagnostics` a publication boundary (test asserts two diagnostics publish once and the outbox stays empty). The adapter now reads command lines with `clojure.edn` on CLJ as well as CLJD (CLJS already used the EDN reader), so command sniffing cannot evaluate its input — the test proves `#=(clojure.core/list (quote help))` no longer becomes the `help` command. `'telemetry` moved out of `local-command-heads` into `rejected-command-heads`, emitting an explicit `:yin.repl.adapter/rejected-command` event rather than no-op'ing locally or shipping the line to the remote evaluator, per the v2 plan's "`(telemetry)` is out in every form".

